  "worked_solution": "To find the coordinates of the points of intersection, we take the equation of the line, replace $x$ and $y$ with the parametric formulas, and then solve for $\\theta :$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{3}x+y & =0 \\\\ \\sqrt{3}(\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{cos}\u2061\\theta )+\\operatorname{sin}\u2061\\theta  & =0 \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3}\\operatorname{cos}\u2061\\theta  & =-\\operatorname{sin}\u2061\\theta  \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}\u2061\\theta  & =-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3}\\operatorname{cos}\u2061\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{\\operatorname{sin}\u2061\\theta }{\\operatorname{cos}\u2061\\theta } & =-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3}\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}\u2061\\theta }{\\operatorname{cos}\u2061\\theta } \\\\ \\operatorname{tan}\u2061\\theta  & =-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe principal value of $\\theta $ is\n\n$$\n\\theta =\\operatorname{arctan}\u2061(-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3})=-\\frac{\\pi }{6}.\n$$\n\nThis value lies outside the domain $0\\le \\theta <2\\pi .$ To get a value that's inside the domain, we add $\\pi :$\n\n$$\n{\\theta }_{1}=-\\frac{\\pi }{6}+\\pi =\\frac{5\\pi }{6}\n$$\n\nTo get a second solution, we add $\\pi $ to ${\\theta }_{1}:$\n\n$$\n{\\theta }_{2}=\\frac{5\\pi }{6}+\\pi =\\frac{11\\pi }{6}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our solutions are $\\frac{5\\pi }{6},\\,\\frac{11\\pi }{6}.$",
